WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.768
-Welcome back to "Late Night".
We're here with Billy Porter.

00:00:02.768 --> 00:00:05.137
You went on a 25 city tour?
-Yes.

00:00:05.137 --> 00:00:06.439
-And how did you feel?

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:08.942
What was your experience
traveling the country?

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:12.912
-It was wonderful
to be back as myself.

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:15.582
You know, I've become famous
playing characters,

00:00:15.582 --> 00:00:17.383
so it was nice
to be back as myself.

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:18.851
It was also very nice --

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:20.786
You know, Brandi Carlile
talks about this.

00:00:20.786 --> 00:00:24.457
She travels all over the world,
and the people that she meets,

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:28.194
you know, she -- she realizes
that America is not as divided

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:30.730
as our news cycles
might make us think.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:34.000
And that was so --
it was so special

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.537
to just go out and touch people
and reach people and hug people

00:00:38.537 --> 00:00:40.273
and give the world
a big bear hug.

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:43.042
And just, like, you know,
I just wanted to bring joy

00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:44.811
and hope again and peace again

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:47.346
and, you know, make people
talk to each other again.

00:00:47.346 --> 00:00:49.715
You know, we've been through
this collective trauma

00:00:49.715 --> 00:00:52.952
where we've, you know,
where we've separated,

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:54.887
and it's time to sort of
come back together.

00:00:54.887 --> 00:00:57.924
And so, the -- the concert
really did that for me.

00:00:57.924 --> 00:01:01.227
It was 25 cities
and five weeks,

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:04.597
and people came,
which was fabulous.

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:08.501
And, you know,
audiences from age 9 to 90,

00:01:08.501 --> 00:01:12.971
all races and colors and creeds
and sexualities and genders,

00:01:12.971 --> 00:01:15.008
it was -- it was magical.

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:17.009
It was amazing, and I can't wait

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:18.511
to go back out.
-That's exciting.

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:21.013
So, that's a lot of shows
in a very short period of time.

00:01:21.013 --> 00:01:22.982
Did your energy ever flag,
or did you just ride

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:24.884
that enthusiasm of the audience?
-Well, I've been doing

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:26.552
eight shows a week
for over 30 years.

00:01:26.552 --> 00:01:28.087
That's my wheelhouse.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:29.322
-You know,
everybody was concerned.

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:31.057
I'm like,
"That's not the problem."

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:32.725
-[ Laughs ]
-The problem ain't me

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:35.562
doing the show, the problem
is my budget ain't big enough.

00:01:35.562 --> 00:01:37.196
[ Laughter ]

00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:40.099
Don't worry about the show.
I got that part.

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:41.767
-[ Laughing ]
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:44.036
-You mentioned writing
all the songs in your album.

00:01:44.036 --> 00:01:46.772
What is your creative process?
Are you collaborative?

00:01:46.772 --> 00:01:49.208
Do you do it fully on your own?
-No, no, no, no, no, no.

00:01:49.208 --> 00:01:50.977
I can't do it on my own.
I come from the theater.

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:53.646
It's a very collaborative
art form.

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:55.915
You know, I write scripts,
I write plays,

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:57.383
I do some of that by myself.

00:01:57.383 --> 00:02:00.052
I also have a writing partner
for that.

00:02:00.052 --> 00:02:02.788
But with the music,
I don't play any instruments.

00:02:02.788 --> 00:02:05.959
So I need a crew.

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:07.693
And this time, it was great

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:11.197
because unlike
my first experience,

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:13.199
these people were actually
listening to me.

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:14.934
-Right.
-And listening to what it was

00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:16.803
that I want to do --
wanted to do.

00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:21.207
It was Justin Tranter, the late,
great Andrea Martin,

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:23.842
Emily K. and Jade Thirlwall
over in London,

00:02:23.842 --> 00:02:26.613
and, you know, we would just
sit, and we would talk,

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:28.781
and then, we would just
build and layer.

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:31.083
I'm a -- I'm an ideas guy,

00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:34.721
I'm a lyric guy,
I'm a top line melody guy.

00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:37.923
And then, all the other stuff,
you know, falls into place.

00:02:37.923 --> 00:02:40.860
It was really great.
I had such a great time.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.664
And these artisans really
helped me with my vision,

00:02:45.664 --> 00:02:47.533
and helped me realize

00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:52.405
this really specific
and individual vision.

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:53.840
-Were you there
for the whole process?

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.374
Was there ever a time
where you, like, went home

00:02:55.374 --> 00:02:56.910
at the end of the night,
and when you came back,

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:58.744
they'd done work on it
and it felt like,

00:02:58.744 --> 00:03:00.613
you know, the -- the elves
who do the cobbling?

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:02.948
-Yes.
You know, the production part.

00:03:02.948 --> 00:03:05.050
-Yeah.
-That happens in the production.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:06.419
-And that's so amazing.
-Yes.

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:08.421
-I mean, the few times I --
It's not for music,

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:10.089
but it's, you know,
you see an edit of something,

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:11.590
you see graphics
that have been added,

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.927
and it is so stunning to see
what these people can do.

00:03:14.927 --> 00:03:19.165
-It's gorgeous. And you know,
I can't do everything...

00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:20.233
by myself.
-But you come pretty close.

00:03:20.233 --> 00:03:21.634
You come pretty close.
-I come close,

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:24.069
but I can't do everything
by myself.

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:25.938
And I-I don't know.

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:29.175
I like the collaboration process
so much.

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:30.710
I really, really do.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.845
You know, people are
in the room with you.

00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:35.214
I find writing sometimes
to be isolating --

00:03:35.214 --> 00:03:36.715
-Yeah.
-...when you're alone

00:03:36.715 --> 00:03:38.484
and by yourself, and, you know.

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:41.721
So I'm a people person,
and I love to be with my people.

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:44.090
-Well, we're very --
We love to have you with us.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.058
And we thank you so much
for making the time for us.

00:03:46.058 --> 00:03:48.527
Congratulations on the album.

00:03:48.527 --> 00:03:51.363
-"Black Mona Lisa",
November 17th.

00:03:51.363 --> 00:03:53.766
Stick around. We'll be right
back with Darren Aronofsky.

